.TH CLOG_CLOSE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_close \- flush, close, and free a logger
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <clogger.h>

void clog_close(clog logger);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Releases all resources owned by the logger:
  - If the logger is file-backed and this is the last handle sharing
    the underlying file (i.e. all derived loggers have also been
    closed), the file descriptor is closed.  If derived loggers still
    exist the fd remains open and the file's ref-count is decremented.
  - If async logging is enabled (see clog_open_fd_mp(3)) and this is
    the last handle, everything already queued is drained and the
    writer thread is joined before the file descriptor is closed.
  - All attached key=value fields are freed.
  - The internal write buffer is freed.
  - The per-handle state is freed; the shared backing store is freed
    only when the last handle is released.

After this call, the handle must not be used.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle to close.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
clog_close() must not be called while another thread is still making any
call (log_* macros, clog_set_level, clog_set_field and friends,
clog_derive, or another clog_close()) with that same handle. Callers are
responsible for ensuring all other use of a handle has finished before
closing it. Closing one handle concurrently with a sibling handle (root
vs. derived, or two derived loggers) that is still logging is unaffected
by this and remains safe, since that sibling is a different, still-open
handle.
For fd-based loggers (clog_open_fd_mp), the file descriptor is NOT
closed; it is the caller's responsibility.
Asserts if logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale, or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3)
